  <tr><td>Formal arrest</td><td>10 April 2022</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 52A sampling</td><td>5 August 2022</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Prosecution complaint filed</td><td>6 October 2022</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Charges framed</td><td>17 March 2023</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Bail granted (6 of 33 witnesses examined)</td><td>2 November 2026</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The rival positions</h2>
<p>For the applicant it was urged that detention without any realistic prospect of an early conclusion of trial violates Article 21; that his detention between 5 and 10 April 2022 itself called for explanation; that there were irregularities in the sealing, handling and preservation of the substance; and that the Section 52A exercise was initiated late. His antecedents were clean, and the nominal roll recorded satisfactory jail conduct with no other case.</p>
<p>Customs opposed bail on the commercial-quantity embargo in Section 37(1)(b)(ii), defended the documentation of the search and recovery, disputed the alleged irregularities, and pressed the flight risk inherent in a foreign national with no roots in India.</p>

<h2>How the Court navigated Section 37</h2>
<p>Justice Narula surveyed the Supreme Court&rsquo;s bail jurisprudence on prolonged incarceration — including <em>Mohd. Muslim @ Hussain v. State (NCT of Delhi)</em>, where bail on the ground of trial delay was held not to be fettered by Section 37, and <em>Rabi Prakash v. State of Odisha</em>, with its observation that conditional liberty must override the statutory embargo where the trial is slow. The judgment also took account of the counter-current: <em>State of Punjab v. Sukhwinder Singh @ Gora</em>, holding the twin conditions mandatory even in cases of long custody, and <em>Union of India v. Chidiebere Kingsley Nawchara</em>, which reads Article 21 in harmony with the statutory restrictions while cautioning that special statutes cannot become vehicles of indefinite detention.</p>
<p>The decisive move was evidentiary. On the first limb of Section 37, the Court found reasonable grounds for a prima facie belief that the applicant may not be guilty — resting on a prosecution witness&rsquo;s admissions about seal discrepancies and missing handover particulars, the involvement of an official not recorded in the panchnamas, case property kept in a drawer, capsules cut open on 9 April 2022, and Section 52A samples drawn only on 5 August 2022. The Court expressly stated that this satisfaction did not rest only on the period of custody. On the second limb, the clean nominal roll and the absence of any material suggesting a likelihood of reoffending sufficed, with flight risk addressed through safeguards rather than continued detention.</p>

<h2>Conditions calibrated to a foreign national</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Identity and presence.</strong> A verifiable Delhi address, FRRO registration within a week, and monthly attendance at the local police station.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Travel locked.</strong> Deposit of all passports, including expired ones; no departure from India, and no travel outside the NCR without permission.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Financial transparency.</strong> An affidavit of income sources and bank accounts within one week, with sureties verified and a lien noted over surety property.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Diplomatic channel.</strong> The investigating officer must inform the High Commission of Uganda; the order goes to the FRRO and immigration authorities.</div>
</div>
<p>The applicant was admitted to bail on a personal bond of Rs 50,000 with two sureties of the like amount, the trial court being free to relax the two-surety requirement by a written order. Any breach permits Customs to seek cancellation, and the trial court was asked to conclude the proceedings expeditiously.</p>
<div class="note">This article summarises a bail judgment of the Delhi High Court for general information. Observations in a bail order are prima facie and do not decide the trial. Nothing here is legal advice for any individual case.</div>
